t is a small, editor-centric todo.txt manager for plain text task lists.

It is intentionally minimal: tasks are lines of text, the file is the
source of truth, and no metadata or background services are involved.


OVERVIEW

t provides a thin wrapper around a single todo.txt file.

It is designed for users who prefer to work directly in text editors
and want predictable behavior over feature-rich task systems.


TASK MODEL

Each task is a single line of text.

Common conventions (not enforced):

- priorities written as (A), (B), (C), ...
- completed tasks prefixed with "x "

All other content is free-form.


LISTING AND LINE NUMBERS

"t list" displays tasks sorted for convenience.

The displayed numbers are the original file line numbers.
Operations using numbers (for example: "t done 3") always refer to
these file line numbers, not to display order.


MARKING TASKS AS DONE

Tasks can be marked as done:

- by file line number
- by matching a text pattern

Only one task is modified per invocation.


EXAMPLE USAGE

t add "(A) Finish article about aerc + postfix + mbsync"
t add "(B) Review SlackBuild for bsddialog"
t add Write short note about nvi workflow

t list
t done 2
t done bsddialog
t push


DEPENDENCIES

- POSIX shell (/bin/sh)
- coreutils
- grep, sed, awk, sort, nl


INSTALLATION

install -m 0755 t ~/.local/bin/t
# or system-wide:
install -m 0755 t /usr/bin/t


